{
  "requirement": "No prompt, tool result, or tool error the harness gives the model MUST state a\nline number or line range of a project source, except within source text it\nquotes and where a tool error repeats a line number the model supplied. The\nharness MUST identify a source location by file and outline selector under\n{cite}`requirements.references.outline_selectors` instead.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no test-adequacy gap for `requirements.authoring.no_line_numbers`. The obligations and the tests that check them are below.\n\n**Obligations**\n1. Prompts must state no line number or range of a project source. This covers the mandatory inputs and the site-prompt matches.\n2. Tool results must state none.\n3. Tool errors must state none, except where an error repeats a number the model supplied.\n4. Source locations must be identified by file and outline selector, per `requirements.references.outline_selectors`.\n\n**Prompts**\n- `src/authoring/inputs.rs#mod[tests]/fn[anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs]` asserts the exact mandatory-input JSON. It has file, item, hash, kind and text and no line fields.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[site_reviews_use_their_prompt_and_record_applies_and_the_site]` asserts the site subject message equals the approved template filled with selector-only matches. It also checks that the message reaches the model.\n- `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[sites_are_the_innermost_nodes_of_site_matches_and_violations_within_them]` asserts the full site-match and violation-match JSON (`matches_value`). That JSON carries an item selector and no line numbers. This covers the violation path that an earlier archived review reported as unmarked. It is now marked.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[submission_description_tests]/fn[submission_descriptions_explain_arguments_and_successful_completion]` checks that the submission tool descriptions tell the model to use outline selectors and never line numbers.\n\n**Tool results**\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms]` asserts that item reads and line-range reads carry no `line_start` or `line_end`.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[line_range_reads_record_the_innermost_covering_nodes]` asserts the same for range reads over Rust and plain-text files. These return selectors and no line fields. Recorded inputs also have no line fields.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules]` and `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[separate_windows_in_one_node_are_divided_by_an_elision_line]` assert that ripgrep entries and lines have no line fields. The second test compares whole entries, so any extra line field would fail it.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs]` asserts that outline output is exactly selector, kind, name and children.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` asserts that `references` sites are exactly name, role, file and item.\n- `ast_grep` results go through the same entry-building code as ripgrep (`SearchNodes::entries`). ast_grep also has its own tests, and the tool description says results have no line numbers.\n\n**Tool errors**\n- `src/authoring/line_numbers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_drop_the_source_line_of_a_parse_error]` checks `without_source_lines` on each error form.\n  - Parse errors, query diagnostics and duplicate-declaration cross references have their numbers removed. They become `file#`, the empty outline selector.\n  - TOML and YAML errors are covered the same way.\n  - The model-supplied \"not an inclusive range\" forms, with a single number or a range, are kept unchanged. This is the stated exception.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_give_the_model_no_line_numbers_it_did_not_supply]` is an end-to-end check.\n  - An out-of-range read repeats only the supplied start number.\n  - A parse error from the tool names its line, but the request actually delivered to the model by `assess` contains \"cannot parse\" and no line number. This confirms the sanitizer is wired into delivery.\n\n**Judgment**\nEvery clause is checked with assertions that would fail if a line field or number leaked. The exceptions are checked too: model-supplied numbers in errors, and quoted source text, which the tests leave intact. The file-and-selector replacement is checked by the `file#` outputs and the selector-only result shapes. I found no unmarked relevant test and no `{fixme}` that needs acknowledging.",
  "references": "5f07d59d3d70ca6a503110d2a797133c2b9f1eafd54fb53e7ee2fb02587ec51a",
